//compile_error: the runtime's internal type names are not type spellings.
//The mirror the compiler builds of the runtime's builtin data types keeps
//its node attached to the tree root — registry identity, tree ownership —
//but yields no unqualified name: `Int32`, `Type` and `Void` are display
//names, the identifiers the runtime tables stringize from the node kind and
//some rendered diagnostics print (§1.4), not spellings of the language. The
//mirrors used to index the global namespace, so each field below resolved —
//`Void v;` to a type with no value representation, `Type t;` to a metaclass
//the instantiation-descriptor layer defines no spelling for — and such a
//shape either degraded silently or was refused only once the image was
//already running. The keyword spellings are untouched: `int`, `void` and
//`string` resolve pre-parsed through runtime-node identity, with no name
//lookup on the path at all.
class C {
    public Int32 a;
    public Type t;
    public Void v;
}

int main() {
    return 0;
}
